 Pattern 5 — Sorting & Binary Search
CORE IDEA: A sorted structure lets you find things in O(log n) instead of O(n). Every database index you create is this principle applied at scale.
Binary Search Template
// Works on ANY sorted array. O(log n) — 1M items = ~20 steps.
function binarySearch(array $arr, $target): int {
    $lo = 0; $hi = count($arr) - 1;
    while ($lo <= $hi) {
        $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi, 2);
        if ($arr[$mid] === $target) return $mid;  // found
        if ($arr[$mid] < $target)  $lo = $mid + 1; // search right
        else                       $hi = $mid - 1; // search left
    }
    return -1; // not found
}
Sorting Algorithms — Know These Three
Algorithm	Time	Space	When to use
usort() / Quicksort	O(n log n) avg	O(log n)	General-purpose. PHP usort() uses this internally. Use for track/playlist ordering.
Merge Sort	O(n log n) stable	O(n)	When you need STABLE sort (preserves tie order). Use for ranking where ties matter.
Counting / Bucket Sort	O(n + k)	O(k)	When values are bounded integers — e.g., sorting tracks by BPM (40–200 range).
Why Your Database Indexes = Binary Search
Every CREATE INDEX in your migrations is the database building a sorted B-tree so it can binary-search instead of full-table scan. This is exactly why you added:
-- From 004_file_metadata.sql — each index enables O(log n) lookup
CREATE INDEX idx_file_metadata_hash_sha256  ON file_metadata(hash_sha256);
CREATE INDEX idx_file_metadata_scan_status  ON file_metadata(scan_status);
CREATE INDEX idx_file_metadata_owner        ON file_metadata(owner_type, owner_id);
-- Without these: O(n) full scan on every findByHash() call
-- With these:    O(log n) B-tree lookup — fast even at 10M rows

