Rules for the Sessions
•	No copy-paste. Type every solution from scratch. The muscle memory matters.
•	After solving, ask: "What is the Big O?" and "Where does this pattern already exist in my codebase?"
•	For every project task, write it in PHP first (your home language), then try it in Python (Money Mike's language) so you can collaborate more fluidly.
•	Use var_dump() and dd() liberally — understanding what's in memory at each step IS the skill.
•	When you get stuck, draw the data structure on paper first. Algorithm problems are visual before they are code.
 Quick Reference — Patterns at a Glance
Pattern	Key data structure	Time complexity	Reach for it when...
Hash Map	Array / Dictionary	O(1) read/write	Deduplication, counting, fast lookup by key
Sliding Window	Two pointers + array	O(n)	Processing contiguous sequences, streams, chunked data
Recursion/Tree	Stack (call stack)	O(n) or O(n log n)	Nested structures: folders, XML, taxonomies, version chains
BFS	Queue	O(V + E)	Shortest path, "within N hops", level-by-level traversal
DFS	Stack / recursion	O(V + E)	All paths, deep exploration, cycle detection
Binary Search	Sorted array	O(log n)	Searching sorted data, range queries, index-backed DB lookups
Merge/Quicksort	Array	O(n log n)	Ordering tracks, playlists, leaderboards, ranked results

Decision Tree — Which Pattern?
Need fast lookup?  →  Hash Map
Processing a sequence/stream with fixed window?  →  Sliding Window
Data is nested / hierarchical?  →  Recursion + Tree Traversal
Finding connections / shortest path between things?  →  BFS / DFS
Data is sorted, or you need it sorted?  →  Binary Search / Merge Sort
Need to rank / order a collection?  →  Sorting + custom comparator

The algorithm is the thinking. The code is just the record of it.
